WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.270
- What connects ancient
African drum circles

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.910
to this Coachella performance by Beyonce?

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.530
And what do historically black
colleges and universities

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:09.420
have to do with it?

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.160
If you've seen the 2002 film drum line,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.950
you know this electric
feeling all too well.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.860
From hip hop inspired beats
to innovative dance routines,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.190
black colleges have been
laying down classic beats

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.490
and grooves for years that
set them apart from the rest.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.050
Today, we're gonna take
a look at the roots

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.830
of HBCU drum lines

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.865
and find out why this percussive
party is so entertaining.

00:00:30.865 --> 00:00:33.532
(drum rattling)

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.143
When the movie "Drumline" came
out, I was in seventh grade,

00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:43.540
And when the school had
went on a field trip,

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:44.880
but my class wasn't going, I didn't go.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.930
I was like, wait, I'm the
drummer though, in the school.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.420
I mean, you see, in this movie though,

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.700
I end up seeing a movie
three times in the theater.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.590
That's the one movie that

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.707
don't nobody wanna watch with me.

00:00:53.707 --> 00:00:56.570
'Cause I'ma say every single word.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.834
- I was in eighth grade when
the movie "Drumline" came out,

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:01.820
I went to Chapel Hill Middle School.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.270
Chapel Hill Middle School is the feeder

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.000
of Southwest DeKalb High School,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.300
which is the actual band for
the Atlanta AT&amp;T Drumline,

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.350
where they were actually
students that got paid

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.740
all the playing parts.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.630
All the cadences were parts

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.580
of the Southwest DeKalb Band Program.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:18.890
- That was a high school band?

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:19.870
- That was a high school band.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.640
- To understand how we got here,

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.460
we have to take a look at the
origin of black marching bands

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:25.293
as a whole.

00:01:25.293 --> 00:01:27.150
Throughout America's military history

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:30.060
many African-Americans were
drafted into the military

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.330
without property of career
or a formal education.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.070
The union used these black
soldiers to drum up support

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.670
for the war and inspire people to enlist.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.720
Groups of smaller military bands

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.290
would travel to community
events, to play patriotic music.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:45.760
They performed for malicious

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.200
to inspire them for the perils of war.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.080
Francis Johnson was a
leader amongst these men

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.020
and laid groundwork for
black military musicians.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.700
He is the first African-American
to have his composition

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.800
published as sheet music.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.500
In 1837, he was also the first
African-American musician

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.000
to tour in Europe.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.100
During the civil war,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.570
there were around 185,000
black men enlisted

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.370
when the war ended, many
uneducated black musicians

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:12.730
were left with nowhere to go.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.100
So they kept doing what they
knew how to do to get by.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.460
Minstrel brass bands
popped up to play music

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.310
to move and entertain the crowd.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:22.100
Minstrelsy is a controversial
performance form

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.550
where stereotypically African attributes

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.580
are emphasized for entertainment.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.715
This blatantly racist form of performance

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:30.860
was often the only way black musicians

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.450
could earn any type of living.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.850
By the time World War I came around

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.450
groups like the Harlem
Hellfighters had emerged.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.620
Officially known as the
369th infantry regiment.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.240
They mainly consisted of
black soldiers and recruits

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:44.510
from Puerto Rico.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.940
The Hellfighters were well-known
for their military band

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:49.060
led by James Reese Europe.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.400
The band not only performed in battle,

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.800
but also toured Europe,
playing jazz and ragtime tunes.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.570
During the war, the Hellfighters
helped introduce Europe

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:58.860
to black American music.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.440
Despite the cultural importance,

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.380
the Harlem Hellfighters
spent the most time

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.700
in continuous combat in
any other American regimen.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.740
They suffered the most casualties too.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.870
But where did school
marching bands come from?

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.730
For that we have to go back
to after the civil war,

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:15.410
after emancipation schools
for African-Americans

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:16.890
were established in the south.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:20.830
Today, these institutions are called HBCU

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:24.350
or Historically Black
Colleges and Universities.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:28.827
In the south the music
culture is so prevalent.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.010
It's so thick.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:35.180
It's still strong it's
like kids can grow up,

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.360
going to see a battle of the bands

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.110
and being influenced by that.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:43.770
Why do HBCU play such a big
role in marching band culture?

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.320
- The history of marching
band is very militant

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.510
is very straight forward, it's commands.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:52.600
The military bands were
there to help the military.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.930
We've got to give credit to
Dr. William Foster at Florida,

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:59.930
A&amp;M really who kind of changed
the way HBCU bands appear

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.340
and how they perform.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:05.531
And it gives us a sense of belonging.

00:04:05.531 --> 00:04:08.364
(trumpet buzzing)

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:18.670
We hear our music.

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:20.420
We produce our music,

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:24.250
but to put it on a field
with live instruments

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:27.040
gives us that seasoning

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.830
that a lot of other bands cannot give.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:36.010
- Music directors like WC
Handy, and Dr. William P. Foster

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.530
shaped the early band sound

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.670
by incorporating African music traditions.

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:41.260
Because of segregation

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:43.500
these bands developed independently

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.720
and created a unique sound centered

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.300
and African-American culture.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.210
There are three primary
styles of marching bands,

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:53.290
military, corps and show style.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:54.760
The style we're talking about today,

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.610
and the one most often used
by HBCU bands is show style

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:00.740
also sometimes called traditional.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.900
Show style band tradition
is rooted in the foundations

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:07.790
of African-American music
like blues and jazz.

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:09.580
Syncopation is the concept of this

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.840
placing the stress notes in music.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.070
For example, Mary had a
little lamb is stressed

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:18.230
in a classical European
approach with no syncopation.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:19.920
Whereas something like this cadence

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.123
has distressed on the off beats.

00:05:22.123 --> 00:05:24.790
(drum rattling)

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:30.910
Call and response is the
idea of musical conversation.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:34.100
You play something and I
play back in response to you.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.630
This comes from traditional
African drumming circles,

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:40.170
where the music was improvised
and felt as a push and pull

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.400
between the players.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:45.540
African vocality is everything
you've heard in r&amp;b, rap,

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:49.560
jazz, and blues, all the
runs, the rhythmic speech,

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:50.740
the vocal effects.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.730
This can even be felt on a
drum line with no singing

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:56.475
through chance and call outs.

00:05:56.475 --> 00:06:01.475
(drum rattling)
(scat singing)

00:06:06.576 --> 00:06:08.240
HBCU drum lines are also unique

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:10.130
with their style of composition.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:12.620
If we look at the corp
style, percussion battery,

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:15.510
only three instruments are
represented, snare drum,

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.850
bass drum and multi-tom's
also referred to as tenors.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.490
The additional tenor drum

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.740
adds for a driving
rapid fire middle voice,

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.800
and they use marching symbols
to add some top end flair,

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:28.623
both audibly and visually.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.560
You may have also noticed

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:32.500
that the equipment used is different.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.800
Corp style and modern military drum lines

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.880
use rigid harnesses that make carrying

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:38.800
and playing the drum much easier.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:42.880
HBCU drum lines, opt to
use straps and slings

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.880
for an increased range of motion,

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.670
letting them incorporate dances

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:48.550
and physical stunts into their plane.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:50.960
One staple of HBCU bands

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.620
is a dance routine called the breakdown.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:56.940
Dr. William Foster of FAMU's Marching 100

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.860
laid the groundwork for the breakdown.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.200
The breakdown is a
special part of the show

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.340
where the band dances and plays a mashup

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:04.960
of current popular music.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.880
Today, HBCU drum lines are
thriving with dedicated events

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.450
like the Honda Battle of the Bands,

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:13.730
the National Battle of the
Bands and other regional events.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:16.400
HBCU drum lines have even
influenced pop music.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.590
So Beyonce, she pretty much raised me

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:22.520
like the first CD I ever
bought with my own money

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:23.520
was writings on the wall.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.086
But what was your reaction when
Beyonce reached out to you?

00:07:27.086 --> 00:07:29.920
- (chuckles) That's a whole story.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:31.410
I'ma try to condense it.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:34.850
I quit my corporate job in February, 2018.

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:36.650
I wasn't really being fulfilled.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.450
So a month later I get
a call from Don Roberts,

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:43.030
who is the owner and CEO of Drumline Live

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:46.417
and he says, "Hey, I have an opportunity.

00:07:46.417 --> 00:07:48.648
"You have to leave for two months,

00:07:48.648 --> 00:07:51.247
"but it's paid it's
for a very big artists.

00:07:51.247 --> 00:07:54.567
"I cannot tell you who
it is, but I promise you,

00:07:54.567 --> 00:07:56.260
"it will be worth it."

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:57.520
I said to my homeboy, I'm like,

00:07:57.520 --> 00:07:58.620
hey man, you got that call from Don.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:00.220
He's like, "Yeah, I got the call."

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:02.350
I was like, I haven't heard anything.

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:03.670
Do you know anything about it?

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:06.740
And he's like, "Man, all I
know is for being Beyonce."

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.470
I say who?

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:10.440
He was like, "Yeah, it's for Beyonce."

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:11.947
I said, "Huh?"

00:08:12.927 --> 00:08:14.130
I was like, "Okay."

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.861
- I have like rehearsal
footage in my mind to Beyonce.

00:08:16.861 --> 00:08:18.820
What was the energy like?

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:19.780
What was the vibe like?

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:23.450
- Honestly speaking truly and honest,

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:27.660
it felt like band
practice every single day.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.496
- Like Beyonce is legendary
Coachella performance,

00:08:30.496 --> 00:08:35.496
other pop stars like Lil NAS
X have incorporated HBCU bands

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:36.463
in their art.

00:08:36.463 --> 00:08:40.340
Right now there are 107
HBC use out of the nations,

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:43.580
approximately 5,300 universities.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:46.680
The culture of the HBCU
drum line goes hand in hand

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:48.839
with the plights of
African-Americans today.

00:08:48.839 --> 00:08:52.130
The constant fight to be
relevant when your slice of pie

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.380
is the smallest has pushed these groups

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:58.890
to create something and refined
much like jazz in its day,

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:02.029
Drumline is a cultural phenomenon.

00:09:02.029 --> 00:09:04.696
(drum rattling)

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:10.573
Before you go,

00:09:10.573 --> 00:09:12.530
I wanna tell you about
a new documentary series

